  "prompt_text": "The figure sketches a reaction network at a surface, emphasizing how iodine species are transformed through both fast and slow steps. Molecular iodine, I2, sits at the center as the key intermediate. From I2, one pathway involves reaction with water to form HIO, which then rapidly leads to iodate, IO3−. Another pathway shows I2 reacting with bromide to form IBr, and IBr then reacting with water to produce iodate while releasing Br2.  \n\nThe arrows use two styles to compare kinetics: solid arrows mark fast reactions, while dashed arrows indicate slower ones. A vertical pair of arrows above I2 also suggests exchange between interfacial and gas or bulk phases, with one direction faster than the other. Overall, the scheme suggests that once I2 is present at the interface, it can either hydrate and oxidize toward iodate or be diverted by bromide into mixed iodine–bromine chemistry through IBr.  \n\nThe main message is that bromide changes the fate of iodine by introducing an alternative, apparently fast interfacial route that still ends in iodate formation but also generates bromine.",
